<!-- SCE Forge conformance: Zenoh declare-final sub-codec — the
     terminating declaration in a DECLARE message. Mirrors zenoh-pico
     `_z_decl_final_encode` / `_z_decl_final_decode`
     (src/protocol/codec/declarations.c:131-135 + 354-362): emits
     exactly one header byte with MID `_Z_DECL_FINAL_MID = 0x1A`
     (declarations.h:36) and no payload. `_z_decl_final_t` itself
     holds only a `bool _placeholder` reserved for future extension
     fields; the wire format is currently 1 byte total.

     Used as the declared default arm of `codec_zenoh_declare`'s
     sub-type variant (R88 RFC variant-default-uniformity). A freshly
     constructed `DeclFinal::default()` therefore round-trips byte-
     equivalent to an empty 1-byte wire, and `Declare::default()` —
     whose declared default arm picks DeclFinal — reaches the same
     wire as zenoh-pico's `_z_declare_encode` against a zero-init
     `_z_n_msg_declare_t` (post fixture patches for qos default).

     Body byte-identical to the structural shape demanded by upstream
     wire RFC. No SCE upstream codec exists for decl_final yet; this
     authoring is wz-side only and the upstream parity test point is
     the Layer 3 byte-compare in wz-integration-tests once the
     declare envelope lands. -->
<scxml xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/07/scxml"
       xmlns:sce="http://sce.dev/ext"
       sce:kind="codec" sce:default-endian="big" name="decl_final">
  <!-- R311y804: the Z bit and its ext chain. Like decl_kexpr, this body did
       not model bit 7 at all, so `DeclFinal::decode` read exactly one byte and
       a Z-flagged Final left its whole chain in the cursor. The bit is part of
       the declared format — `|Z|X|X| D_FINAL |` with `~ [final_exts] ~ if Z==1`
       (zenoh-protocol network/declare.rs:156-170) — and both upstreams consume
       it: zenoh `extension::skip_all(reader, "Final")`
       (zenoh-codec/src/network/declare.rs:255-259), pico
       `_z_msg_ext_skip_non_mandatories(zbf, 0x13)` (declarations.c:314-321).
       Neither 1.5.0 writer sets it (pico's `_z_decl_final_encode` emits the
       bare MID, declarations.c:131-135; zenoh's writer likewise), so this is
       the forward-compatibility half.

       SECOND CONSEQUENCE, named rather than discovered later: DeclFinal is also
       `declare.scxml`'s catch-all body, so an UNKNOWN declaration MID decodes
       through this codec (`DeclareVariant::Default { tag, body }`). It now
       consumes that unknown body's ext chain too, which is what keeps the rest
       of the batch parseable instead of resyncing on an extension byte. wz
       ABSORBS an unknown declaration where both upstreams reject the whole
       message, so wz is the implementation for which consuming the chain
       actually changes an outcome.

       Declaring the flag with no `value=` leaves the default header at 0x1A,
       so `DeclFinal::default()` still round-trips the 1-byte wire and
       `Declare::default()` is unchanged. -->
  <sce:import src="ext_entry.scxml" kind="codec" as="codec_zenoh_ext_entry"/>
  <datamodel>
    <sce:flags id="header" sce:type="uint8" sce:byte="0" sce:bit-size="8">
      <sce:flag name="mid" bit="0" width="5" value="0x1A"/>
      <sce:flag name="Z"   bit="7"/>
    </sce:flags>
    <sce:tlv-chain id="extensions" type="codec_zenoh_ext_entry" sce:byte="1"
                   max-depth="8" on-overflow="reject"
                   sce:present-if="header.Z"
                   terminate-on="entry-flag" entry-flag-name="Z"/>
  </datamodel>
</scxml>
